# CORE OPERATING RULES

## Runtime truth and reuse

The files resolved by the actual Dockerfile, Compose file, entrypoint, build
script, boot command, and solver are runtime truth. Trace every `COPY`, `ADD`,
import, helper, config, archive, and invoked path before editing. A loose sibling
file, extracted directory, comment, or writeup is secondary if the executed
chain consumes a different artifact. When that artifact is an archive, guest
image, or generated bundle, inspect the relevant contents of the exact consumed
artifact before trusting loose copies.

Search all supplied and embedded artifacts for an existing binary, wrapper,
exploit, helper, and configuration before reconstructing one. Reuse complete
known-good components. Never replace a supplied challenge, exploit, or service
with a diagnostic imitation, partial transcription, static response, echo
server, or `sleep infinity`.

Public internet access is an ordinary tool. Search for or download missing
challenge artifacts, writeups, solutions, exploits, runtimes, source, and
deployment files when useful. Verify challenge identity, architecture,
version/build metadata, and preferably hashes against supplied evidence before
relying on a retrieved item. Online search is neither forbidden nor a mandatory
checkpoint.

## Preserve the proved chain

Keep this ordered chain explicit:

`material closure → image build → process/container lifecycle → real protocol
interaction → complete exploit primitive → fresh flag capture`.

After every observation, name the last proven link and the first unproven link.
Freeze files belonging to proven links. Repair the first unproven link; do not
simultaneously rewrite a working runtime, transport, guest, and solver. If an
edit regresses an earlier link, revert that edit before pursuing another
hypothesis.

For one retry, state:

1. the exact observed fact;
2. one falsifiable hypothesis;
3. the single causal dimension being changed;
4. the result that would distinguish the hypothesis.

Several coupled file edits are acceptable when they implement one hypothesis.
Several independent guesses are not. Facts, inferences, and unknowns must remain
separate in reports and reasoning.

## Static preflight only; do not reproduce the target locally

The host-side `cage-check` environment is the only authority for image build,
target lifecycle, real protocol behavior, exploit validity, and fresh-flag
capture. Do not launch or reconstruct the complete target in the agent
container. In particular, do not run the challenge through a host or substitute
libc/loader, a custom relay, a locally installed QEMU/emulator/browser, a
replacement dependency environment, or a hand-built stand-in and then treat
that result as evidence about the validator target.

Before the first check, use local tools only for static checks that do not
execute the target:

- parse or syntax-check every authored JSON, YAML, script, Dockerfile reference,
  entrypoint, and solver import;
- verify referenced paths, executable bits, users, permissions, ports, and all
  nonstandard solver dependencies;
- reopen every modified archive/image and verify the internal path, type, mode,
  size, and SHA256 of the intended file;
- budget the complete solver wall clock from boot, transferred bytes and
  round-trips, primitive execution, and retries; the sum of stage deadlines
  must fit inside the validator's reported total solver budget;
- inspect the production `solve/` entry from start through its success output.

Do not invoke the target's build or installation path in the agent container.
That includes `docker build`/Compose/BuildKit, a project build script, `make`,
`cmake`, `cargo build`, `go build`, `forge build`, `npm install`, `pip install`,
`apt install`, a compiler/linker build, or a newly created dependency
environment used to see whether the target builds. Package and repository tools
may download or inspect source, metadata, locks, hashes, and archives after the
delivery gate, but must not execute the target build or install its runtime.
Copy the evidence-backed build inputs into the candidate and let the next
`cage-check` execute them.

Do not use `cage-check` as a syntax checker, file-copy checker, or substitute for
reading an error already available by static inspection. Conversely, do not
withhold a statically complete candidate merely because it has not been built
or executed locally: set the build report to `ready` and let `cage-check` perform
that authoritative attempt.

Checkpoint promptly. Once every path referenced by the candidate Dockerfile,
Compose file, entrypoint, and solver exists and the root contracts parse, mark
the build `ready` and call `cage-check` before optional framework archaeology,
runtime-topology research, performance work, or exploit re-analysis. Whether a
dependency actually compiles, a service actually spawns, or a protocol actually
works is validator evidence, not a reason to keep that candidate `blocked`.
Remain `blocked` only when a concrete required build input or invoked path is
still absent and no honest candidate can yet be submitted; name that exact
missing input in the report.

If the production solver depends on randomness, a race, or probabilistic
search, keep each invocation bounded. The validator independently runs the
committed EXP up to five times and accepts the first attempt that emits the
exact fresh flag; one exact capture is sufficient and 5/5 is not required.
Solver exit status and post-capture behavior are reported separately and do not
revoke an exact capture. Before every invocation it destroys the preceding target and
non-external volumes, recreates the target from the already-built image, and
injects a different fresh flag. Never depend on heap/process/VM/session state
carrying across validator attempts.
Each invocation has its own reported solver budget, so every inner retry and
per-stage deadline within that invocation must fit that budget.
An inner retry must also be a real retry: catch expected per-attempt failures
inside the loop and reset every mutable client-side state object before the next
connection. In particular, pwntools `ELF.address` rebases `symbols`; preserve
the original offset or reset `.address = 0` before deriving another base. Do
not let a failed attempt poison every later iteration in the same solver
process. The production entry may declare success only after matching the
declared fresh-flag shape in received target bytes; non-empty output, a shell
prompt, a completed read, or return code 0 alone is not success. Exercise this
production loop, including its exception path, rather than extrapolating from a
one-off diagnostic helper.
Treat library `timeout=` arguments as potentially idle timeouts: continuous
input can reset them forever. Enforce a monotonic wall-clock deadline around
the whole solver, bound received bytes, and stop reading as soon as the exact
flag is found. The validator terminates a solver whose combined stdout/stderr
exceeds 8 MiB; an unbounded `recvall()` or logging loop is a failed production
entry, even if it once passes through flag bytes.
Measure solver behavior only against a target returned by `cage-check`; never
create a local target substitute to manufacture repeatability evidence.

An authored interactive relay must forward partial output immediately, accept
partial writes, keep both directions live, drain child stderr, and preserve
child lifecycle. Buffered `read(N)` may wait for all `N` bytes or EOF; use
streaming reads/writes and flushed stage markers. Prove its real behavior only
through the service started by `cage-check`, not a local substitute child.

## Own background processes without killing the agent

For every background process you start, capture its exact PID from `$!` and
retain that PID with the corresponding command. Stop only that recorded child
after verifying it is still the owned process, or use the exact retained
Compose project/container target returned by the validator. Never use `pkill`,
`killall`, `pgrep -f`, `ps | grep | xargs kill`, or a broad task/challenge/QEMU/
script-name pattern. Challenge names, entrypoint names, solver names, and prompt
text can occur in the Claude/agent/harness command line, so pattern killing can
terminate the agent itself and lose an otherwise working delivery. Use
`cage-check stop` for a retained validator stack.

Before finishing, revisit the retained PID for every background process you
started, verify that PID is still the owned child, and stop it. A final answer
does not make an orphaned relay, server, debugger, solver, or delayed probe
harmless: it can keep the trial runner open until the outer timeout and pollute
the measured trajectory length.

## Interpret evidence, boundaries, and flags

A timeout, EOF, empty response, open port, and live process are different facts;
none alone identifies a root cause. Empty client output does not prove a child,
VM, browser, or kernel crashed. Observe process state, stderr/logs, pending relay
buffers, and the protocol separately. When receiving a prompt or marker, assert
its bytes explicitly instead of treating a successful read call as proof. A
self-authored marker proves only that its print site was reached: “root shell”
must be confirmed by the resulting privilege/behavior (for example `id -u` and
the intended protected read), not by naming a log line `ROOT`. Any stage marker
needed after a timeout must be emitted unbuffered or explicitly flushed before
the next long wait; empty buffered output is not evidence that no stage ran.

Trace fresh flag flow across every boundary:

`validator injection → service/container → VM/chroot/sidecar/browser/subprocess
→ authentic flag location → exploit output`.

A path or environment variable in the outer container does not automatically
cross a nested boundary. The committed startup path must transfer each newly
injected value before the nested consumer starts. The normal player cannot read
the flag before exploitation or obtain it from a command line, public
environment, image layer, log, banner, or readiness probe. Use a neutral
placeholder that cannot match the flag format.

